
# llm-host-guard: keep dev containers off the LAN (Docker bypasses ufw INPUT).
# Append to /etc/ufw/after.rules, adjust interfaces + ports, then `sudo ufw reload`.
#
# Two things that are easy to get wrong:
#  1. Docker rewrites the destination port BEFORE this chain runs (published 54322 -> container 5432), so a plain
#     `--dport 54322` rule matches nothing unless the container listens on the same number. Match the port the
#     client dialled with `-m conntrack --ctorigdstport` instead.
#  2. Add the rule for EVERY interface that faces a network (e.g. both Ethernet and Wi-Fi): `ip -br addr`.
# Then test from ANOTHER device (PowerShell: Test-NetConnection <ip> -Port <port>); testing on the host proves nothing.
*filter
:DOCKER-USER - [0:0]
-A DOCKER-USER -i enp100s0 -p tcp -m conntrack --ctorigdstport 54321:54327 --ctdir ORIGINAL -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i wlp98s0 -p tcp -m conntrack --ctorigdstport 54321:54327 --ctdir ORIGINAL -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i enp100s0 -p tcp -m conntrack --ctorigdstport 11235 --ctdir ORIGINAL -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -i wlp98s0 -p tcp -m conntrack --ctorigdstport 11235 --ctdir ORIGINAL -j DROP
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN
COMMIT
